      "text": "**Short answer:** your DNA data does **not** show a clear caffeine-sensitivity flag, but it also doesn\u2019t fully rule one out. I\u2019d use an **8-hour caffeine cutoff** as your default experiment.\n\nWhat I found in your genome run:\n- No curated caffeine-related finding for `CYP1A2`, `ADORA2A`, `AHR`, or `POR`.\n- `CYP1A2` showed **0 variants**, mean depth ~25.9\u00d7, and no low-coverage exons.\n- `ADORA2A`, `AHR`, and `POR` were not available as gene-summary rows in this run, so I can\u2019t confidently assess caffeine anxiety/sleep sensitivity from those genes.\n\n**Practical recommendation:**  \nIf your target bedtime is ~11 PM, stop coffee/caffeine by **3 PM**. If you\u2019re prone to insomnia, anxiety, or waking overnight, make it **noon** for two weeks and track sleep latency + awakenings.\n\nWhy: controlled trials show that **400 mg caffeine** can disrupt sleep even **6 hours before bedtime**, and newer dose/timing work suggests lower doses are less disruptive while larger doses need a longer runway. ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24235903/?utm_source=openai))\n\nSo I\u2019d frame this as: **you don\u2019t have an obvious genetic \u201cbad caffeine handler\u201d signal, but your best cutoff should be phenotype-tested.** Try:\n1. **2 weeks:** caffeine only before noon, max 100\u2013200 mg/day.  \n2. If sleep doesn\u2019t improve, you can probably tolerate later coffee.  \n3. If sleep improves, keep the earlier cutoff.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
